<h2>The shape of the allegation</h2>
<p>The FIR, lodged in 2022, concerned alleged forgery around land pattas in Udaipur, invoking the full document-offence suite of the IPC &mdash; cheating (420), forgery of valuable security (467), forgery for the purpose of cheating (468), using a forged document as genuine (471) and conspiracy (120-B). The applicant&rsquo;s connection to the documents was indirect: the patta in question stood in the name of his wife, who had passed away. He himself was not named in the FIR.</p>

<h2>Interim protection used well</h2>
<p>The order is a textbook illustration of how interim protection should be deployed. The court had granted the applicant protection on 12 August 2026; in the three months that followed, he joined the investigation and cooperated. When the matter returned for final disposal, the Public Prosecutor &mdash; while formally opposing bail &mdash; could not dispute either that the patta was not in the applicant&rsquo;s name or that he had joined the investigation. Cooperation converted interim protection into a track record, and the track record carried the final order.</p>

<h2>The court&rsquo;s calculus</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>FIR silence.</strong> Absence from the FIR is never conclusive, but combined with the absence of any specific allegation it shifts the justification burden to the prosecution.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No custodial need.</strong> In document-driven offences, the record is largely on paper; the court found no material showing custodial interrogation was required.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parity.</strong> The principal accused and other patta holders were already on bail &mdash; regular or anticipatory &mdash; through orders of 2025 and 2026.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Conduct.</strong> Three months of cooperation under interim protection, with no complaint of misuse.</div>
</div>

<h2>What practitioners can take from it</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Build the cooperation record.</strong> Attend every call of the investigating officer during interim protection and document it; the final hearing is won in the interregnum.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Map the co-accused.</strong> A parity chart &mdash; who got bail, when, and with what role &mdash; is often the most persuasive single page in a pre-arrest bail file.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Mind the conditions.</strong> Travel abroad now requires leave of the court; breach of any condition invites cancellation and colours every future application.</div>
</div>
<p>Delhi courts apply the same grammar under Section 482 BNSS: vicarious implication in family land documents, without a named role, remains one of the stronger fact patterns for pre-arrest protection &mdash; provided the applicant&rsquo;s conduct during the proceedings stays beyond reproach.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports an order of the Rajasthan High Court for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
